=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
A thorough end-to-end investigation of the VLAN 20 path for PC11 (172.16.20.11) finds no fault in the current state of the lab. PC11's upstream port — switch2 eth5 — is UP/LOWER_UP, assigned to bridge br_vlan20 in forwarding state, with no STP blocking (STP is disabled on both bridges), zero RX/TX errors, and PC11's MAC (22:3d:b8:d6:8f:4c) correctly learned in the FDB. Switch2's startup script consistently assigns eth3–eth6 to br_vlan20 without any conditional logic that could skip eth5. No ebtables or iptables rules exist on switch2.

At the mswitch0 layer, vlan20@br0 is a proper 802.1Q VLAN-20 subinterface on the switch fabric bridge, carrying IP 172.16.20.254/24 and visible in the ARP table with PC11's correct MAC. OSPF is distributing all required routes.

Live ping tests confirm PC11 can reach: its gateway (172.16.20.254, 0% loss), VLAN 20 neighbours PC9 and PC10 (0% loss), inter-VLAN gateways 172.16.10.254 and 172.16.30.254, and cross-VLAN host 172.16.10.5. The reverse direction (PC9 → PC11, PC10 → PC11) also succeeds.

The symptom reported in ticket #8129 is not reproducible. The most likely explanations are a transient startup-ordering race (the Kathara container veth pairs may not have been fully ready when switch2's startup loop ran, briefly leaving eth5 unmastered) or a Kathara container restart that re-ran the startup script and self-corrected. No configuration change is required; the network is operating correctly.
=== END ===